**Delivery plan COMPLETE (2026-07-20, 51 PRs):** every slice in the spec's delivery plan is shipped + verified on `prod`. Final native-UI surface landed: **Slice 5 Android Jetpack Compose UI** (#47/#49) — `RadioScreen`/`RadioViewModel`/`PlaybackService` (Media3 MediaSessionService), TalkBack semantics + custom actions per §6.3. Can't install Android SDK locally (slow link truncates), so it's **CI-verified**: new `android-app` job (setup-java 17 + android-actions/setup-android + gradle/actions/setup-gradle → `gradle :app:compileDebugKotlin`). Two CI iterations to green (issue #48): (1) ported missing `formatDuration` to Kotlin core; (2) renamed `setActiveChannel`→`selectChannel` to dodge a JVM setter-signature clash with the `activeChannel` state property. **CI is now 6 jobs, all green on dev+prod:** Linux sandbox, Linux a11y/playwright, macOS Swift build (iOS SwiftUI), Linux libsignal (E2EE DM), Linux Kotlin core, Linux Android-SDK Compose compile. **Only genuinely-external work remains (never faked, marked ⬜ in STATE.md):** on-device VoiceOver/TalkBack *runtime* (needs device/simulator — screens+a11y already compile-verified), GPU throughput (CPU whisper/piper shipped, speed-only), and provider accounts (email/SMS for auth delivery, Twilio number for IVR). Nothing buildable-without-a-provider is left.

**Beta cut (2026-07-20):** feature-complete build marked **v0.8.2 BETA** — root `package.json` version 0.8.2, STATE.md beta banner, git tag `v0.8.2-beta` on `prod`, GitHub **pre-release** live (github.com/Taha-Mahmoodi/third-eye-world-mono/releases/tag/v0.8.2-beta). Narrative going forward (user-set): beta done → **closed beta testing** → tester feedback drives changes that stay private until **stable v1.0** ships publicly → v1 in active dev at v0.8.2, target end of Aug 2026. Companion: updated the public spec on sadqi's [[third-eye-world-paper-v2-update]] (README §status + §12 timeline) to say the same — authored as Said, commit a41ca34. **Honesty boundary held:** wrote the closed-beta status at the roadmap level (details withheld until v1) but invented NO fake tester metrics/quotes/counts; on-device screen-reader runtime + provider accounts still ⬜ in STATE.md, never faked.

**v1 line started (2026-07-20)** — branch **`v1`**, kept separate from the frozen v0.8.2-beta line (`dev`/`prod`) at Taha's instruction. Product pivot: a **dating-app-style card deck** (swipe right = like · right-twice-fast = like & next · swipe left = skip · press-and-hold = record). Taha chose this knowing it **reverses non-negotiable principles 5 & 6** (I surfaced the conflict; he picked "literal swipe-first" and stated his view that the gestures are more accessible/adoptable for blind users). Shipped + CI-green into `v1`: **A** card deck (`SwipeDeck`/`MemoCard` + pure `lib/gestures.ts`, #58), **B** press-and-hold record with tap-toggle twin (#59), **C** onboarding wizard that teaches *and drills* each gesture three ways (#60), **STATE ledger** (#61). **Accessibility floor I held throughout (do not drop):** every gesture action ALSO has a labelled button + keyboard shortcut + aria-live announcement, press-hold always keeps a tap twin, persistent media-transport RadioPlayer stays mounted — because VoiceOver/TalkBack *consume raw swipes*, so buttons/keys are what actually reach a blind user. Reversal + unresolved engineering dissent recorded publicly in spec **§4.1**. **Never claim the gestures are proven more accessible** — no screen-reader user has driven the deck on-device; that's a leadership view, not a measured result. Gotchas: branch names must be `v1-*` (a `v1/...` ref can't coexist with branch `v1`); CI needed `v1` added to `pull_request` triggers **pushed straight to `v1`** (GitHub reads triggers from the PR's *base*); local e2e runs `next start` so **rebuild before `pnpm e2e`**; don't bind Playwright locators to the wizard dialog's name (it changes per step).

Spec (source of truth) is the v2.0 paper on sadqi's account: [[third-eye-world-paper-v2-update]]. Delivery order per spec: web (Next.js) first, then native iOS + Android (E2EE DMs ship with the phone apps). Ten non-negotiable design principles gate every feature.

